<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Airlangga</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1002–1049</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Airlangga was a Javanese king who founded the kingdom of Kahuripan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> around 1002, son of King Udayana and the Javanese princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahendradatta">Mahendradatta</a>, he married into the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharmawangsa">Dharmawangsa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medang-kingdom">Medang</a> and survived its destruction in 1016. He rebuilt royal power from 1019, reunited eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> and before his death around 1049 divided his realm between Janggala and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kediri-kingdom">Kediri</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/airlangga.jpg" alt="Airlangga" width="320" height="511" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gunawan Kartapranata · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Airlangga.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Airlangga</b>, son of Udayana and Mahendradatta and the dead king's nephew, escaped to the Vanagiri forest, rallied support and in 1019 re-established the kingdom, including Bali, as Kahuripan, its capital near the Brantas estuary.</blockquote><a href="/empire/medang-kingdom">Medang Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>King Udayana married the Javanese princess Mahendradatta, and their son <b>Airlangga</b> later ruled Kahuripan in Java.</blockquote><a href="/state/bedulu-kingdom">Bedulu Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Janggala arose around 1045 from <b>Airlangga's</b> partition of Kahuripan, which gave one son the eastern lands around the Brantas delta, the old capital Kahuripan and the coastal ports near modern Surabaya and Sidoarjo.</blockquote><a href="/state/janggala">Janggala</a></li><li><blockquote>When Haji Wurawari destroyed the court of Dharmawangsa Teguh of the Medang Kingdom in 1016, his young son-in-law <b>Airlangga</b>, a prince from Bali, fled to the forest with a few followers.</blockquote><a href="/state/kahuripan">Kahuripan</a></li><li><blockquote>Kediri, also called Panjalu, was the western half of the realm <b>Airlangga</b> divided around 1045, with its capital at Daha on the Brantas River.</blockquote><a href="/state/kediri-kingdom">Kediri Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
